  "result": "Acinetobacter baylyi starvation-induced genes identified through incubation in long-term stationary phase. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20511417/)\nciteturn43search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.5 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; PMID: 20511417 ...   * PMC: PMC2901722 ...   * Research Support, Non-U.S. ... The genes encode metabolic, gene expression, DNA maintenance, envelope, and conserved hypothetical proteins. ...   * Cell.\n# Acinetobacter baylyi starvation-induced genes identified through incubation in long-term stationary phase.\n\nApplied and environmental microbiology\n\nPMID: 20511417\n\nPublication date: 2010 Jul\n\nArticle date: 2010 05 28\n\nPublication status: ppublish\n\nJournal abbreviation: Appl Environ Microbiol\n\nJournal country: United States\n\nJournal NLM unique ID: 7605801\n\nJournal linking ISSN: 0099-2240\n\nISSN: 1098-5336\n\nVolume: 76\n\nIssue: 14\n\nPages: 4905-8\n\n## Identifiers\n\n  * DOI: 10.1128/AEM.01806-09\n  * PII: AEM.01806-09\n  * PMC: PMC2901722\n\n## Authors\n\n  * C Phoebe Lostroh\n  * Bruce A Voyles\n\n## Affiliations\n\n  * Department of Biology, Colorado College, Colorado Springs, Colorado 80903, USA.\n\n## Languages\n\n  * eng\n\n## Publication types\n\n  * Journal Article\n  * Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't\n\n## Abstract\n\nAcinetobacter species encounter cycles of feast and famine in nature. We show that populations of Acinetobacter baylyi strain ADP1 remain dynamic for 6 weeks in batch culture. We created a library of lacZ reporters inserted into SalI sites in the genome and then isolated 30 genes with lacZ insertions whose expression was induced by starvation during long-term stationary phase compared with their expression during exponential growth. The genes encode metabolic, gene expression, DNA maintenance, envelope, and conserved hypothetical proteins.\n\n## MeSH terms\n\n  * Acinetobacter / growth & development\n  * Acinetobacter / metabolism\n  * Acinetobacter / physiology\n  * Artificial Gene Fusion\n  * Gene Expression Profiling / methods\n  * Gene Expression Regulation, Bacterial\n  * Genes, Reporter\n  * Lac Operon\n  * Stress, Physiological\n  * beta-Galactosidase / genetics\n  * beta-Galactosidase / metabolism\n\n## Substances\n\n  * beta-Galactosidase (Registry number: EC 3.2.1.23)\n\n## Grants\n\n  * Agency: Howard Hughes Medical Institute; Country: United States\n\n## References\n\n  * Science. 1993 Mar 19;259(5102):1757-60; PMID: 7681219\n  * Genetics. 2003 Aug;164(4):1271-7; PMID: 12930738\n  * J Bacteriol. 2001 Nov;183(21):6288-93; PMID: 11591672\n  * J Bacteriol. 2000 Aug;182(15):4361-5; PMID: 10894750\n  * J Bacteriol. 1969 Apr;98(1):281-8; PMID: 5781579\n  * Cell. 1996 Jul 26;86(2):181-4; PMID: 8706122\n  * Mol Syst Biol. 2008;4:174; PMID: 18319726\n  * Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2002 Jun 25;99(13):8737-41; PMID: 12060704\n  * Nucleic Acids Res. 2004 Oct 28;32(19):5766-79; PMID: 15514110\n  * Nat Rev Microbiol. 2006 Feb;4(2):113-20; PMID: 16415927\n  * J Bacteriol. 1999 Sep;181(18):5800-7; PMID: 10482523\n  * Appl Environ Microbiol. 2008 Feb;74(4):1273-5; PMID: 18083886\n  * Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 1999 Mar 30;96(7):4023-7; PMID: 10097156\n  * J Bacteriol. 1995 Mar;177(5):1336-47; PMID: 7868609\n  * Appl Environ Microbiol. 2007 May;73(10):3327-32; PMID: 17400787--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProteomic Analysis of Methanococcus voltae Grown in the Presence of Mineral and Nonmineral Sources of Iron and Sulfur (https://doi.org/10.1128%2Fspectrum.01893-22)\nciteturn43search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.3 years ago; Crawled: last month; Proteomics samples were analyzed at the Nevada Proteomics Center University of Nevada, Reno, which receives support from the Nevada INBRE (IDeA Network of Biomedical Research Excellence), under a National Institutes of Health (NIH) program (GM103440). ... File (spectrum.01893-22-s0001.xlsx) ... https://doi.org/10.1128/spectrum.01893-22\n## ACKNOWLEDGMENTS\n\nThis work was supported by the U.S. Department of Energy, Office of Science, Basic Energy Sciences under Award DESC0020246 to E.M.S., J.B.B., J.D., and B.B. Proteomics samples were analyzed at the Nevada Proteomics Center University of Nevada, Reno, which receives support from the Nevada INBRE (IDeA Network of Biomedical Research Excellence), under a National Institutes of Health (NIH) program (GM103440).\n\n## Supplemental Material\n\nFile (spectrum.01893-22-s0001.xlsx)\n\n  * Download\n  * 596.34 KB\n\nASM does not own the copyrights to Supplemental Material that may be linked to, or accessed through, an article. The authors have granted ASM a non-exclusive, world-wide license to publish the Supplemental Material files. Please contact the corresponding author directly for reuse.\n\n### Information\n\n#### Published In\n\nImage: cover image Microbiology Spectrum\n\nMicrobiology Spectrum\n\nVolume 10 • Number 4 • 31 August 2022\n\neLocator: e01893-22\n\nEditor: Kristen M. DeAngelis, University of Massachusetts Amherst\n\nPubMed: 35876569\n\n#### Copyright\n\n© 2022 Steward et al. This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International license.\n\n#### History\n\nReceived: 24 May 2022\n\nAccepted: 17 June 2022\n\nPublished online: 25 July 2022\n\n#### Keywords\n\n  1. methanogen\n  2. iron-sulfur cluster\n  3. proteomics\n  4. pyrite\n\n#### Topics\n\n  1. Environmental Sulfur Cycling\n  2. Iron Cycling and Reduction\n  3. Iron Metabolism\n  4. Metabolism and Biogeochemical Cycling\n  5. Metal Ion Homeostasis\n  6. Microbial Ecology and Evolution\n  7. Microbial Physiology and Genetics\n  8. Regulation of Metal Ion Homeostasis\n  9. Sulfur Assimilation\n  10. Sulfur Assimilation in Anaerobes\n  11. Sulfur Cycling\n\n#### Authors\n\n##### Katherine F. Steward\n\nDepartment of Chemistry and Biochemistry, Montana State University, Bozeman, Montana, USA\n\nView all articles by Katherine F. Steward\n\n##### D